    {
      "id": "54c3039b-7e62-4d16-b144-2dc6572f363c",
      "name": "Sticky Note",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        -560,
        -16
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "width": 384,
        "height": 1040,
        "content": "### How it works\n\nCompanies in Japan have to keep every employee's overtime under three ceilings at once: a hard monthly cap, an average across any 2 to 6 consecutive months, and a yearly total. Whoever handles HR usually works this out in a spreadsheet near the end of each month, and the rolling average is the one that gets missed.\n\nEmployees report their overtime and holiday work for the month through a form. On a schedule the workflow reads every month on file and, for each person, works out how many hours are left under each ceiling. The tightest one wins. Anyone whose remaining hours fall below the threshold is posted to Slack together with the limit that is binding, and the warning is appended to a log. If everyone has room, the manager still gets a one-line confirmation.\n\nIf a past month is missing, that average is skipped and the gap is named in the alert, so the number is never inflated by guessing. Nothing here decides whether someone may work more. It only says how much room is left.\n\n### Setup steps\n\n1. Add your Google Sheets and Slack credentials.\n2. Create a spreadsheet with two tabs, MonthlyHours and WarningLog, using the column names shown in the sheet nodes.\n3. Paste the spreadsheet ID into the two config fields: the Configure limits node and the spreadsheetId field in Normalize submission.\n4. Set the Slack channel name in Configure limits and invite the bot to that channel.\n5. Adjust the limits and the warning threshold if your agreement differs.\n6. Share the form URL, run once manually, then activate."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "f27c283f-60bd-4747-82c4-bc1124d7be79",
      "name": "Sticky Note1",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        -96,
        -224
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": "#FFFFFF",
        "width": 1360,
        "height": 496,
        "content": "### Collect monthly hours\nEmployees report one month at a time. Employee ID and month form the match key, so resubmitting the same month overwrites the row instead of adding a second one. Obvious mistakes are sent straight back to the form with a plain explanation."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "30a72890-0bfa-4971-b975-aefe363f845d",
      "name": "Sticky Note2",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        -112,
        576
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": "#FFFFFF",
        "width": 688,
        "height": 496,
        "content": "### Read what has been reported\nThe limits live in one place, so a different agreement is a one-line change. The current month is derived here rather than typed, and every month in the sheet is read at once because the rolling average needs history."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "32108328-c5c7-443c-89e0-9087c00ea1e6",
      "name": "Sticky Note3",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        608,
        576
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": "#FFFFFF",
        "width": 432,
        "height": 496,
        "content": "### Find the binding limit\nThe code works out each person's room under the monthly cap, every 2 to 6 month average and the annual total, and keeps the tightest. Averages missing a month are skipped and named. One item is always returned, so the next step runs even when nobody is close."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "00a9da57-2bf3-4b09-98bf-a48830d0ed1a",
      "name": "Sticky Note4",
      "type": "n8n-nodes-base.stickyNote",
      "position": [
        1056,
        448
      ],
      "parameters": {
        "color": "#FFFFFF",
        "width": 688,
        "height": 624,
        "content": "### Warn and log\nEach warning names the limit that is binding, not just a number, so the manager knows which rule to watch. Both branches reach Slack, so silence is never mistaken for success. Every warning is appended to the log for the record."
      },
      "typeVersion": 1
    },
    {
      "id": "725702b6-da5a-4fd9-bd00-3478f5a6ca86",
